# MASTER GIT — 20 Essential Git Commands
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
| No. | Git Command       | Purpose                                                      |
| --- | ----------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------ |
| 01  | `git init`        | Initialize a new Git repository                              |
| 02  | `git clone`       | Clone an existing repository                                 |
| 03  | `git status`      | Check the working tree status                                |
| 04  | `git add`         | Stage changes for the next commit                            |
| 05  | `git commit`      | Save staged changes as a commit                              |
| 06  | `git push`        | Upload local commits to a remote repository                  |
| 07  | `git pull`        | Fetch and integrate changes from a remote                    |
| 08  | `git fetch`       | Download remote updates without integrating them             |
| 09  | `git branch`      | List, create, or manage branches                             |
| 10  | `git switch`      | Switch between branches or create and switch to a branch     |
| 11  | `git checkout`    | Switch branches or restore files (legacy, versatile command) |
| 12  | `git merge`       | Merge changes from one branch into another                   |
| 13  | `git rebase`      | Replay commits on top of another base                        |
| 14  | `git log`         | View commit history                                          |
| 15  | `git diff`        | Compare changes between files, commits, or states            |
| 16  | `git stash`       | Temporarily save uncommitted changes                         |
| 17  | `git reset`       | Move `HEAD` or unstage changes; can discard changes          |
| 18  | `git revert`      | Create a new commit that undoes an earlier commit            |
| 19  | `git cherry-pick` | Apply a specific commit to the current branch                |
| 20  | `git tag`         | Create or list tags, often used for releases                 |
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------


## Quick command examples ##

    01. git init
    02. git clone <repository-url>
    03. git status
    04. git add .
    05. git commit -m "Your commit message"
    06. git push origin main
    07. git pull origin main
    08. git fetch origin
    09. git branch
    10. git switch main
    11. git checkout main
    12. git merge feature-branch
    13. git rebase main
    14. git log --oneline
    15. git diff
    16. git stash
    17. git reset HEAD~1
    18. git revert <commit-hash>
    19. git cherry-pick <commit-hash>
    20. git tag v1.0.0


Important distinctions to remember:

- `fetch` downloads updates; `pull` downloads and integrates them.
- `switch` is focused on branches; `checkout` can also restore files.
- `reset` can rewrite local history; `revert` safely undoes changes through a new commit.
- `merge` combines histories; `rebase` replays commits onto a new base.

Recommended learning order: Learn commands 01–08 first, then branches and integration (09–13), followed by history, undoing changes, and releases (14–20).
